The stuff that might be stored in the NVRAM might include:

* Time zone information.

* Which RAM blocks to disable (usually due to bad RAM).

* Management ID, which is 16-bits. This is normally zero and would not
normally be useful, but there are some potential uses.

* Default boot device and partition. (It can also be configured to boot
to ROM BASIC, and it is possible to force booting to ROM BASIC by holding
the appropriate key combination before the operating system starts.)

* Security settings for operating system and ROM BASIC. These will be
used to permanently disable some features (e.g. the hard drive), until
the computer is restarted.

* RTC reference (0 means TAI; other numbers are currently undefined).

There might also be internal jumpers (or switches) to switch the default
NVRAM bank, and this can also be switched by the CPU (XOR by the default
bank number), as well as the ability for the CPU to lock the access until
the computer is restarted. These internal jumpers/switches might also be
used to disable NVRAM changes, and to disable some features when using
the ROM BASIC.
